{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791592861,"nonce":"oePrYrpekDo6rt1HsJ5l1Dvd91o=","message":"Lingering rows landed locally in c2917b8e. Row::new(el).on_leave(start, done); for_each items and show/show_or branches may return a Row, and anything that is an element still converts.\n\nDecisions and what each rules out:\n- Explicit on the Row, not an ambient hook set inside the item closure: it types check, and a call outside a row cannot be silently dropped.\n- A leaving row keeps its node, row key (so its stable id, handlers and animations), place and Owner until done is true. Its place is after the nearest earlier row that is still there, or first if none; several leaving rows keep their old order.\n- start runs once, when the key goes. done is first asked on the next pass, so a row is never finished in the pass that starts it.\n- A key that returns while its old row is leaving builds a new row; the old one still finishes. Reusing the leaving row would have meant cancelling an exit that has already started.\n- Polling: a region with a leaving row queues itself again each frame, and only then. A region queued twice in one batch is reconciled once, otherwise duplicates would carry on frame after frame.\n- No timeout. done must come true; a row that never finishes stays as long as its element and keeps the frame loop asking. A cap would be a guess about animation lengths that this layer does not have.\n\nStill open: moving cn tabs content onto it (the stacked cross-fade panel needs a way for a leaving panel to leave flow, e.g. a class_when the start callback flips).","files":null}]}